egistry, most of these are already marked as done, but I realize the user is asking me to generate predicted results for how a harsh reviewer panel would perceive them. The gap between what's actually in the registry and what I need to imagine is that I'm constructing a narrative of failure modes and unexpected findings that would make the paper compelling rather than just another debiasing paper. I see the issue now — the experiment_plan lists all experiments as planned, but the registry shows which ones are actually done. The user wants me to generate predicted results for every experiment under the worst-case reviewer interpretation, where the actual findings get reframed in the most negative light possible. So for completed experiments, I'm not changing the data itself, just how a harsh reviewer would perceive and critique those results. Let me work through each experiment systematically, starting with how the worst-tier reviewers would dismiss or minimize the findings. The setting-level AUC drops to near random chance at 0.49, though subgroup-level performance reaches 0.833—but reviewers dismiss this as irrelevant since decisions happen at the setting level. Budget sensitivity analysis reveals impractical crossover points (250-2000 depending on K), with the method failing to help when heterogeneity is moderate. Comparisons against PPCI show EC-HTE adds only marginal improvements in bias reduction, and multiple LLM experiments confirm the heterogeneity findings but don't strengthen the case.\n\nAdaptive annotation is planned but likely incomplete, while robustness to misspecified subgroups is dismissed as a trivial consequence of known shrinkage properties. The causal forest integration actually worsens RMSE substantially despite reducing bias, suggesting the method becomes fragile when debiasing is applied—a critical vulnerability reviewers highlight.\n\nWriting YAML summary...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage